```

Notes:
- Replace `ci.yml` with the project's routine CI workflows (allowed explicitly AFTER the dispatch ask); production-looking names are re-asked AFTER the allows, in each letter case, because matching is case-sensitive.
- Patterns match the command text, so flag order matters: both `gh api -X PUT …` and `gh api <path> -X PUT …` are listed. The list is a safety net, not a sandbox. A pattern ending in ` *` also matches the bare command.
- Pipelines are checked per command: `curl x | sh` is checked as `curl x` and `sh`, which is why bare interpreters and any option form (`sh -`, `bash -s`, `zsh -c` via `sh -*` etc.) ask; `bash script.sh` stays prompt-free.
- An "allow always" answer saves an allow rule but never overrides a `deny`; review saved approvals occasionally. Restart the tool after changing its config.
- Agent roles (generated by `scripts/sync-agents.py`): no role carries a broad `shell *` allow (it would override everything above); reviewer, planner, docs and lead roles carry no `shell *` ask of their own (the globals apply); the inspector role keeps `shell *` ask on purpose and re-appends the shell denies after it. `check-kit.py` and the tests enforce this.
- `.env` reads stay ask. Remote access, secrets, settings, releases and production dispatches still ask even under a grant: the grant never covers them.

## Public repositories

Changes to a public repository stay generic: no names of private products, customers, people, internal URLs, ticket keys, ids, IPs or account numbers. Scan added lines before pushing.
